<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CYPHRA-SOC Sysmon configuration — a working default, not a starting sketch.

  Install (elevated):  sysmon64.exe -accepteula -i sysmon-config.xml
  Update in place:     sysmon64.exe -c sysmon-config.xml
  Show what is live:   sysmon64.exe -c

  schemaversion 4.90 needs Sysmon 15.0 or newer. On an older binary the install
  fails with "schemaversion not supported" rather than silently degrading — if that
  happens, either update Sysmon (the link in SYSMON_SETUP always serves current) or
  drop this to 4.82 and delete the FileExecutableDetected group, which is the only
  thing here that 4.82 lacks.

  ── The rule this file is written to ──────────────────────────────────────────

  An exclusion is only safe if an attacker cannot satisfy its match condition. That
  single test decides every filter below:

    * Excluding by *full path* under C:\Windows\System32 is safe — writing there
      already requires the privilege the exclusion would have been used to obtain.
    * Excluding by *filename* is not. `Image condition="image"` matches on the leaf
      name, and renaming a binary costs an attacker nothing.
    * Excluding by *domain suffix* is safe for suffixes the attacker cannot
      register (microsoft.com, windowsupdate.com) and unsafe for anything with a
      customer-controlled subdomain (blob.core.windows.net, s3.amazonaws.com,
      *.workers.dev), which is exactly where modern C2 lives.

  So process creation (event 1) excludes nothing at all. It is the single
  highest-value event Sysmon produces — the named log source for 264 of the 697
  ATT&CK techniques that carry detection guidance — and every published exclusion
  for it filters on a path or a command line, both of which the adversary chooses.
  The volume is affordable; a blind spot is not.

  Where volume genuinely is unaffordable — image loads, registry writes, file
  creation — the filters are *include* lists rather than excludes. An include list
  that misses something produces a gap that `detect/coverage.py` can see and
  report. An exclude list that is too broad produces a gap that looks like a quiet
  host. Between two kinds of incompleteness, choose the visible one.

  ── Deliberately not enabled ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  * **ClipboardChange (24)** writes the clipboard's *contents* to the archive
    directory. On a host that develops an encrypted messenger that means every
    copied passphrase, recovery code and private key lands in a world-readable
    file. The detection value does not come close.
  * **FileBlockExecutable (27) / FileBlockShredding (28)** do not log — they
    *block*, in the kernel, on a path match. That is a response action, and
    response actions in CYPHRA go through `respond/safety.py` where the blast
    radius is computed and a kill switch exists. Turning one on from a telemetry
    config would put an unbounded, unreviewable prevention rule on the host behind
    the operator's back. The collector maps both events if some other config emits
    them; this one will not be why.
  * **FileDelete (23)** archives every deleted file it matches, which fills the
    disk on a build host in hours. FileDeleteDetected (26) carries the same
    telemetry without the copy and is enabled instead. Event 23 is worth turning on
    scoped to a crown-jewel directory once `enrich/asset.py` knows which
    directories those are.
-->
<Sysmon schemaversion="4.90">

  <!--
    IMPHASH is here for a specific reason: it hashes the import table, not the
    file, so it is stable across recompilation and repacking. It is what clusters
    four droppers into one malware family when all four SHA-256s are unique.
    `parse_hashes` in sysmon.py keeps it in `unmapped` because OCSF has no field
    for it, lower-cased so a hunt joining it against an intel feed matches.
  -->
  <HashAlgorithms>SHA256,SHA1,MD5,IMPHASH</HashAlgorithms>

  <!--
    Certificate revocation checking makes an outbound OCSP/CRL request per newly
    seen signed binary, from the sensor, synchronously. It slows process creation,
    and it tells anyone watching egress the moment a new binary appears. Signature
    *status* is still recorded from the local chain; only the network check is off.
  -->
  <CheckRevocation>False</CheckRevocation>

  <!--
    The one setting here that would corrupt CYPHRA's own telemetry if left at its
    default. Sysmon does a reverse lookup on every network event to fill
    DestinationHostname. Those lookups are real DNS traffic from the sensor, so
    `network_flow` records them as flows and the DNS collector records them as
    queries — the observer's own footprint arriving as observations. Worse, on an
    attacker-controlled nameserver each one confirms that the connection was
    noticed. Resolution belongs in `enrich/`, after the fact, from the lake.
  -->
  <DnsLookup>False</DnsLookup>

  <!--
    Where FileDeleteDetected and ProcessTampering put preserved artifacts. Nothing
    enabled below actually archives file *contents* (see the note on event 23), so
    this stays small; it exists so Sysmon has a valid destination rather than
    falling back to a default the operator did not choose. Lock it down after
    install: icacls C:\Sysmon /inheritance:r /grant:r "SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F" "Administrators:(OI)(CI)F"
  -->
  <ArchiveDirectory>C:\Sysmon</ArchiveDirectory>

  <EventFiltering>

    <!-- ══ 1 — Process creation ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
         No filter, by the rule at the top of this file. An empty exclude group is
         the explicit way to say "log all of it" and survives a later `-c` merge;
         deleting the group entirely would leave the same behaviour with no record
         of the decision. -->
    <RuleGroup name="process creation — unfiltered on purpose" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessCreate onmatch="exclude" />
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 5 — Process termination ════════════════════════════════════════════
         Cheap, and it is what closes a process's lifetime. Without it every
         timeline `correlate/` builds has open-ended intervals, and "the process
         was still running when the beacon called out" becomes unanswerable. -->
    <RuleGroup name="process termination" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessTerminate onmatch="exclude" />
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 2 — File creation time changed (T1070.006 timestomp) ═══════════════
         Installers and updaters do this legitimately and often. Excluded by full
         path only. Deliberately *not* excluding browsers: a browser that
         timestomps a file in Startup is the interesting case. -->
    <RuleGroup name="timestomping" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreateTime onmatch="exclude">
        <Image condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\msiexec.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="is">C:\Windows\SysWOW64\msiexec.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="is">C:\Program Files\Google\Chrome\Application\chrome.exe</Image>
      </FileCreateTime>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 3 — Network connection ═════════════════════════════════════════════
         `network_flow` already sees the packets; what it cannot see is *which
         process* opened the socket, and that attribution is the whole reason to
         run this event as well as capture.

         Excluded: loopback, and the four local-discovery protocols that produce
         constant broadcast noise on any office network. What that costs is
         visibility of LLMNR/mDNS *poisoning* (T1557.001) from this host's own
         point of view — a real technique, detected in CYPHRA from the responder
         side in the DNS collector instead. SMB (445), NetBIOS session (139), RPC
         (135), WinRM (5985/5986) and RDP (3389) are all left in: they are noisy
         and they are also exactly how lateral movement travels. -->
    <RuleGroup name="network connections" groupRelation="or">
      <NetworkConnect onmatch="exclude">
        <DestinationIp condition="is">127.0.0.1</DestinationIp>
        <DestinationIp condition="is">::1</DestinationIp>
        <DestinationPort condition="is">1900</DestinationPort>
        <DestinationPort condition="is">3702</DestinationPort>
        <DestinationPort condition="is">5353</DestinationPort>
        <DestinationPort condition="is">5355</DestinationPort>
      </NetworkConnect>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 6 — Driver load ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Unfiltered. Volume is a handful per boot, and the technique that matters
         here (T1068 bring-your-own-vulnerable-driver) uses a driver with a
         *valid* Microsoft-countersigned signature — so filtering on
         Signed=true would remove precisely the events worth having. -->
    <RuleGroup name="driver load" groupRelation="or">
      <DriverLoad onmatch="exclude" />
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 7 — Image load ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The highest-volume event Sysmon has: thousands per minute, most of them
         signed Microsoft DLLs into signed Microsoft processes. Include-only.

         Three groups: anything not validly signed, then the specific DLLs whose
         load *is* the technique, then loads out of user-writable directories.
         `module_file_sha256` on these events is routed away from
         `process_file_sha256` by the collector — attributing a malicious DLL's
         hash to the host process would invert the T1574 detection it exists for. -->
    <RuleGroup name="image load — unsigned or broken signature" groupRelation="or">
      <ImageLoad onmatch="include">
        <Signed condition="is">false</Signed>
        <SignatureStatus condition="is not">Valid</SignatureStatus>
      </ImageLoad>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="image load — DLLs whose load is the technique" groupRelation="or">
      <ImageLoad onmatch="include">
        <!-- AMSI bypass (T1562.001): the provider DLL loading into a process that
             has no business scanning anything, or being patched after load. -->
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\amsi.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <!-- Unmanaged CLR hosting (T1620) — .NET loaded into a native process. -->
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\clr.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\clrjit.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\mscoree.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\coreclr.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <!-- PowerShell without powershell.exe (T1059.001 unmanaged runspace). -->
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\System.Management.Automation.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\System.Management.Automation.ni.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <!-- Credential access surface (T1003, T1555): DPAPI, vault, LSA, SAM. -->
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\vaultcli.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\samlib.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\cryptsp.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\dpapi.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\wlanapi.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <!-- Remote execution and token theft primitives. -->
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\wmiutils.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\winhttp.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\dsquery.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <!-- Keylogging and screen capture. -->
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\dxgi.dll</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="end with">\taskschd.dll</ImageLoaded>
      </ImageLoad>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="image load — from a user-writable directory" groupRelation="or">
      <ImageLoad onmatch="include">
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\AppData\Local\Temp\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\AppData\Roaming\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\Users\Public\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\Windows\Temp\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\ProgramData\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\Downloads\</ImageLoaded>
        <ImageLoaded condition="contains">\$Recycle.Bin\</ImageLoaded>
      </ImageLoad>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 8 — CreateRemoteThread ═════════════════════════════════════════════
         Classic injection (T1055.002/.003). Low volume; the two excludes below
         are the only legitimate users on a normal desktop and both are
         full-path. -->
    <RuleGroup name="remote thread creation" groupRelation="or">
      <CreateRemoteThread onmatch="exclude">
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\wbem\WmiPrvSE.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</SourceImage>
        <StartModule condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\kernel32.dll</StartModule>
      </CreateRemoteThread>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 9 — RawAccessRead ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Direct volume reads (T1006) — how SAM, SYSTEM and NTDS.dit get copied
         past a file lock. Almost nothing legitimate does this outside of backup
         and defragmentation. -->
    <RuleGroup name="raw disk access" groupRelation="or">
      <RawAccessRead onmatch="exclude">
        <Image condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\dfrgui.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\defrag.exe</Image>
        <Image condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</Image>
      </RawAccessRead>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 10 — Process access ════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Include-only and narrow: this event is expensive at scale. What is worth
         paying for is any handle opened to LSASS (T1003.001) and to CYPHRA's own
         processes, because a SOC agent that can be read or written by a
         non-administrative process is not a control.

         Deliberately *not* filtering on GrantedAccess masks. The published masks
         (0x1010, 0x1410, 0x143a) enumerate what today's dumpers request, so a
         mask filter detects the tools that already exist and nothing else. The
         mask is on the event; let `detect/` reason about it, where the logic is
         versioned and testable. -->
    <RuleGroup name="handles to LSASS and to CYPHRA" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessAccess onmatch="include">
        <TargetImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\lsass.exe</TargetImage>
        <TargetImage condition="end with">\python.exe</TargetImage>
        <TargetImage condition="end with">\veddb-server.exe</TargetImage>
      </ProcessAccess>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="known-benign LSASS readers, by full path only" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessAccess onmatch="exclude">
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\wbem\WmiPrvSE.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\csrss.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\wininit.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\services.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\Windows\System32\MsMpEng.exe</SourceImage>
        <SourceImage condition="is">C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows Defender\Platform\MsMpEng.exe</SourceImage>
      </ProcessAccess>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 11 — File create ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Include-only. Three things are worth the volume: writes to persistence
         locations, executable and script content dropped where a user can write,
         and the artifacts of specific techniques. -->
    <RuleGroup name="file create — persistence locations" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreate onmatch="include">
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Start Menu\Programs\Startup\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\Tasks\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\Tasks\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\GroupPolicy\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\drivers\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\spool\drivers\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\wbem\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Microsoft\Windows\PowerShell\PSReadLine\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Documents\WindowsPowerShell\</TargetFilename>
      </FileCreate>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="file create — executable or script content in a writable place" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreate onmatch="include">
        <!-- Extension pairs the two ways round: a match on either the risky
             extension or the writable directory is enough on its own, because
             groupRelation="or" makes each element independent. Narrowing to
             "risky extension AND writable directory" would need a compound rule
             per pair and would miss a .dll dropped into System32. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.ps1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.psm1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.bat</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.cmd</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.vbs</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.vbe</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.js</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.jse</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.wsf</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.wsh</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.hta</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.lnk</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.scr</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.pif</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.cpl</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.msi</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.msp</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.msc</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.jar</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.iso</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.img</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.vhd</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.vhdx</TargetFilename>
        <!-- Office macro-enabled formats, which is how the chain usually starts. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.docm</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.xlsm</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.pptm</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.xll</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.xlam</TargetFilename>
      </FileCreate>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="file create — technique artifacts" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreate onmatch="include">
        <!-- Credential store copies (T1003.002/.003) and dump files. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.dmp</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">\NTDS.dit</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Temp\SAM</TargetFilename>
        <!-- Ransomware note and staging patterns (T1486). -->
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">DECRYPT</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">RECOVER</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">README_TO_RESTORE</TargetFilename>
        <!-- Archive built for exfiltration (T1560) in a user directory. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.7z</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.rar</TargetFilename>
        <!-- WER and prefetch removal is a defence-evasion signal in itself. -->
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\Prefetch\</TargetFilename>
      </FileCreate>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 12/13/14 — Registry ════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Include-only; unfiltered registry is unusable. `TargetObject` is matched
         with `contains` rather than `begin with` throughout, because Sysmon
         renders per-user keys as HKU\<SID>\… — a `begin with "HKCU"` rule matches
         nothing at all, which is the most common error in a hand-written Sysmon
         config and is silent. -->
    <RuleGroup name="registry — autostart and service persistence" groupRelation="or">
      <RegistryEvent onmatch="include">
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Run</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\RunOnce</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\RunServices</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Explorer\Shell Folders</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Explorer\User Shell Folders</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Winlogon\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Windows\AppInit_DLLs</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Windows\Load</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Windows\Run</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Image File Execution Options\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\SilentProcessExit\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentControlSet\Services\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\Run</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Environment\UserInitMprLogonScript</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Control\Session Manager\BootExecute</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Control\Lsa\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Control\SecurityProviders\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Control\Print\Monitors\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Control\Terminal Server\Wds\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\CurrentVersion\Authentication\</TargetObject>
      </RegistryEvent>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="registry — COM and shell hijack" groupRelation="or">
      <RegistryEvent onmatch="include">
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\InprocServer32\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\LocalServer32\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\TreatAs\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\ShellEx\ContextMenuHandlers\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Shell\Open\Command</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\ms-settings\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\mscfile\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Exefile\Shell\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Folder\Shell\</TargetObject>
      </RegistryEvent>
    </RuleGroup>
    <RuleGroup name="registry — security control tampering" groupRelation="or">
      <RegistryEvent onmatch="include">
        <!-- Defender being turned off or told to ignore a path (T1562.001). The
             exclusion-path key is the one that matters most and is the one least
             often watched. -->
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Windows Defender\Exclusions\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Windows Defender\DisableAntiSpyware</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Windows Defender\Real-Time Protection\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Windows Defender\Features\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Windows Defender Security Center\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Policies\Microsoft\Windows Defender\</TargetObject>
        <!-- Firewall, UAC, ETW, event log, script block logging. -->
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\SharedAccess\Parameters\FirewallPolicy\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Policies\System\EnableLUA</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Policies\System\ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Policies\System\LocalAccountTokenFilterPolicy</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\WMI\Autologger\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Services\EventLog\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\PowerShell\ScriptBlockLogging\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\PowerShell\Transcription\</TargetObject>
        <!-- Cleartext credential caching re-enabled (T1003.001 via WDigest). -->
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\WDigest\UseLogonCredential</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Control\Lsa\RunAsPPL</TargetObject>
        <!-- RDP enabled, and Office macro protections removed. -->
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Terminal Server\fDenyTSConnections</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Office\</TargetObject>
        <!-- Sysmon's own configuration and driver. -->
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Services\SysmonDrv\</TargetObject>
        <TargetObject condition="contains">\Services\Sysmon</TargetObject>
      </RegistryEvent>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 15 — Alternate data stream created ═════════════════════════════════
         Zone.Identifier is mark-of-the-web: its presence on a dropped executable
         is what says "this came from the internet", and its *removal* is T1553.005.
         The stream contents are hashed, so the same file arriving by four routes
         collapses to one artifact in the case. -->
    <RuleGroup name="alternate data streams" groupRelation="or">
      <FileCreateStreamHash onmatch="include">
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Downloads\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\AppData\Local\Temp\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Start Menu\Programs\Startup\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.exe</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.dll</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.ps1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.hta</TargetFilename>
      </FileCreateStreamHash>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 17/18 — Named pipes ════════════════════════════════════════════════
         An SMB beacon is a named pipe (T1572), and PsExec, Cobalt Strike's default
         pipe patterns and Impacket's remote-service execution all show here.
         Volume is moderate; the excludes are the pipes Windows itself creates
         thousands of. Note these are exclusions by *pipe name*, which an attacker
         does control — so each one is a name they could adopt to hide. They are
         accepted anyway, because without them the event is unusable, and
         `detect/` compensates by scoring on the creating process as well. -->
    <RuleGroup name="named pipes" groupRelation="or">
      <PipeEvent onmatch="exclude">
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\Anonymous Pipe</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\PSHost.</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\wkssvc</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\lsass</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\srvsvc</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\netlogon</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\Winsock2\CatalogChangeListener</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\LOCAL\</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\crashpad_</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\chrome.</PipeName>
        <PipeName condition="begin with">\mojo.</PipeName>
      </PipeEvent>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 19/20/21 — WMI subscriptions ═══════════════════════════════════════
         Unfiltered. A permanent WMI event consumer (T1546.003) is rare enough
         that every one of these deserves to be looked at, and it is one of the
         few persistence mechanisms that survives without touching disk or the
         Run keys. -->
    <RuleGroup name="WMI persistence" groupRelation="or">
      <WmiEvent onmatch="exclude" />
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 22 — DNS query ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Process-attributed name resolution: the query, the answers, and which
         binary asked. DNS tunnelling, DGA beaconing and the first move of nearly
         every intrusion are all here.

         Excluded only by suffixes the adversary cannot register. No CDN or cloud
         storage suffix is excluded — blob.core.windows.net, s3.amazonaws.com,
         *.workers.dev and *.trycloudflare.com all take arbitrary customer
         subdomains, so excluding them is handing out a free covert channel. -->
    <RuleGroup name="DNS queries" groupRelation="or">
      <DnsQuery onmatch="exclude">
        <QueryName condition="end with">.microsoft.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.windowsupdate.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.windows.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.msftncsi.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.msftconnecttest.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.office365.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.office.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.microsoftonline.com</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.in-addr.arpa</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="end with">.ip6.arpa</QueryName>
        <QueryName condition="is">localhost</QueryName>
      </DnsQuery>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 25 — Process tampering ═════════════════════════════════════════════
         Process hollowing and herpaderping (T1055.012) — the image on disk no
         longer matches the image in memory. Very low volume, very high value,
         unfiltered. -->
    <RuleGroup name="process image tampering" groupRelation="or">
      <ProcessTampering onmatch="exclude" />
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 26 — File delete detected (no archiving) ═══════════════════════════
         Indicator removal (T1070) and ransomware's own cleanup. Include-only, and
         scoped to content whose deletion is meaningful: executables, scripts,
         logs and shadow-copy artifacts. Deleting a build output is not an
         incident, and on this host there are a great many of those. -->
    <RuleGroup name="file deletion" groupRelation="or">
      <FileDeleteDetected onmatch="include">
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.exe</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.dll</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.sys</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.ps1</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.bat</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.vbs</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.hta</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="end with">.evtx</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\winevt\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Start Menu\Programs\Startup\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\Tasks\</TargetFilename>
      </FileDeleteDetected>
    </RuleGroup>

    <!-- ══ 29 — Executable file detected ══════════════════════════════════════
         Fires when a PE lands on disk, hashed at write time — which closes the
         window where a dropper writes, executes and deletes faster than any
         scheduled scan. Scoped to user-writable directories, where a new
         executable is unexpected; System32 sees new PEs on every patch Tuesday.
         Needs schemaversion 4.90 (Sysmon 15.0+); delete this group for 4.82. -->
    <RuleGroup name="new executable on disk" groupRelation="or">
      <FileExecutableDetected onmatch="include">
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\AppData\Local\Temp\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\AppData\Roaming\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Downloads\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Users\Public\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\Temp\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\ProgramData\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\$Recycle.Bin\</TargetFilename>
        <TargetFilename condition="contains">\Windows\System32\Tasks\</TargetFilename>
      </FileExecutableDetected>
    </RuleGroup>

  </EventFiltering>
</Sysmon>
